<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hermann von Helmholtz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Hermann Ludwig Ferdinand von Helmholtz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hermann von Helmholtz was a German physicist and physician, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> in 1821, whose work ranged across physiology, psychology, physics and philosophy. His 1847 treatise on the conservation of energy is credited as the first formulation of the principle in its most general form. In 1849, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, he measured the speed of the signal along a nerve fibre, and in 1851 he invented the ophthalmoscope for examining the inside of the eye; his Sensations of Tone (1863) influenced musicologists into the twentieth century. After chairs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> he became professor of physics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1871, where his students included Heinrich Rudolf Hertz.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-von-helmholtz.jpg" alt="Hermann von Helmholtz" width="330" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermann_von_Helmholtz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Helmholtz was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, the son of Ferdinand Helmholtz, the headmaster of the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a>, who had studied classical philology and philosophy and was a close friend of the philosopher Immanuel Hermann Fichte. He took a medical doctorate in 1842 and served a one-year internship at the Charité hospital, since medical students received financial support.</p><p>He taught anatomy at the Academy of Arts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1848 and in 1849 became associate professor of physiology at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg">University of Königsberg</a>. In 1855 he accepted a full professorship of anatomy and physiology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, where he was not particularly happy, and three years later he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> as professor of physiology. In 1871 he took his last university post, as professor of physics at the Friedrich Wilhelm University in Berlin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His first major work, the treatise of 1847 On the Conservation of Force, grew from his study of muscle metabolism. Credit for the principle is shared with Julius von Mayer, James Joule and Daniel Bernoulli, but he is credited with its most general first formulation.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> in 1849 he measured the speed of a nerve signal in a frog's sciatic nerve, using a galvanometer as a timing device and reporting speeds of 24.6 to 38.4 metres per second, when most believed signals travelled immeasurably fast. His Handbook of Physiological Optics set out theories of depth, colour and motion perception and became the fundamental reference of the field, and his dispute with Ewald Hering over spatial and colour vision divided physiologists. In Sensations of Tone he described the Helmholtz resonator.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Among his students and associates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-planck">Max Planck</a>, Wilhelm Wien, Albert A. Michelson and Heinrich Rudolf Hertz, who became the first to demonstrate electromagnetic radiation. Wilhelm Wundt, a founder of experimental psychology, built on his sensory physiology. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-graham-bell">Alexander Graham Bell</a>, unable to read German, misread Helmholtz's diagrams of resonators and later said that had he read them he would not have invented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> on the harmonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> principle.</p><p>Helmholtz died on 8 September 1894. His broadly Kantian philosophy of science, including his rejection of Euclidean geometry as the only science of space, inspired new readings of Kant and contributed to neo-Kantianism. Emperor William II raised him to the nobility in 1883, and the Helmholtz Association, the largest German association of research institutions, is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hermann von Helmholtz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
